# HVDC Supervision & Communication Verification Lab

**A training-only software engineering demonstrator for supervisory behavior, industrial communication, automated verification, and engineering change control around a fictional two-terminal HVDC link.**

> **Safety and accuracy boundary:** This repository is not Hitachi Energy MACH software, is not an HVDC converter model, is not a protection relay, and is not suitable for connection to any real plant or OT network. All plant values, limits, delays, identifiers, and test conditions are synthetic. The OPC UA server is a local learning endpoint and is not hardened for network use.

## 1. Why this project exists

This project is designed as a practical learning lab for the entry-level HVDC Control & Protection Software Engineer role in System Supervision & Communication. It exercises the engineering activities named in the role description: software development, testing, verification, system integration, industrial communication, technical documentation, cross-domain interface thinking, and a small virtual commissioning rehearsal.

Hitachi Energy describes MACH as a computerized HVDC control and protection platform that includes station control/monitoring servers, operator workstations, control and protection computers, digital signal processing units, I/O systems, and valve-control units. This project does **not** reproduce those components. It concentrates on a small supervisory-software slice: simulated operating states, alarms, command validation, data quality, status publication, and a local protocol interface.

## 2. What you will build and practise

- A deterministic simulator for a fictional two-terminal DC link.
- A small supervisory state model: `STANDBY`, `RUNNING`, `TRIPPED`.
- Simulated start, stop, power-setpoint, and trip-reset request handling.
- Interlocks based on station readiness, communications health, measurement quality, and telemetry freshness.
- Synthetic alarm and delayed/latching trip behavior for DC voltage, current, communications, measurement quality, stale data, and cooling temperature.
- A local OPC UA server that publishes telemetry/status and accepts simulated command requests.
- Repeatable JSON fault scenarios that generate CSV telemetry and JSON event evidence.
- Unit tests, static analysis, scenario regression, GitHub Actions CI, and an AWS CodeBuild-compatible `buildspec.yml`.
- Requirements traceability, interface documentation, test plans, a verification record, a fictional cross-functional design review, and a virtual FAT-style checklist.

## 3. Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    SIM[Plant simulator] --> ENG[Supervisory / alarm engine]
    ENG --> UA[Local OPC UA server]
    UA <--> CLIENT[CLI client or UAExpert]
    ENG --> CSV[CSV telemetry + JSON events]
    DEV[Developer workstation] --> GIT[Git repository]
    GIT --> GHA[GitHub Actions]
    GIT --> CB[AWS CodeBuild local agent]
    GHA --> EVIDENCE[Verification evidence]
    CB --> EVIDENCE
```

### Approximate training mapping

| Industrial-system concept | Training-project representation |
|---|---|
| Station supervision / monitoring application | `PlantSimulator` state, alarm, and command logic |
| Operator workstation | Bundled OPC UA CLI client or a separate OPC UA client such as UAExpert |
| Plant I/O and measurements | Synthetic telemetry values and data-quality flags |
| Industrial supervisory interface | Local OPC UA server and typed address space |
| Engineering verification | pytest, scenario outputs, interface checks, test records |
| Software configuration/change control | Git branches, pull requests, issues, CI, version tag |

This is an architectural analogy only. It is not a description of Hitachi Energy's internal software architecture, engineering methods, or MACH implementation.

## 4. Technology choices

| Area | Tool | Cost for this learning path | Purpose |
|---|---|---:|---|
| Programming | Python 3.13 | Free | Simulator and command-line tools |
| Editor | Visual Studio Code | Free | Editing and debugging |
| Version control | Git | Free | Commits, branches, change history |
| OPC UA | `asyncua` 2.1.0 | Free / open source | Local OPC UA client/server implementation |
| Tests | pytest + coverage | Free / open source | Automated verification and coverage |
| Static checks | Ruff | Free / open source | Linting and common error checks |
| Hosted CI | GitHub Actions (public repo) | Free for standard runners in public repositories | Run checks on pushes and pull requests |
| AWS build familiarity | AWS CodeBuild `buildspec.yml` + local build agent | No AWS account or AWS build minutes for local execution; Docker uses local resources | Execute the same build phases in a CodeBuild-style environment |
| Optional packet inspection | Wireshark | Free / open source | Inspect the local OPC UA session |

A separate AWS account is **not** needed to complete the core project. A public GitHub repository is recommended so standard GitHub-hosted Actions runners remain free under GitHub's current policy. Do not enable usage-based paid services just to complete this lab.

